The table below covers the standard ME/CFS co-prescription set. Most rows are pharmacological inferences — no study has tested pramipexole against these agents in any fatigue population. Rows with a literature anchor are marked; the rest state "no documented interaction" explicitly rather than implying safety from silence. The "Net direction" and "ME/CFS note" columns are the decision-aid content: pharmacological inferences from receptor-class reasoning (D1 stimulation, D2 modulation, alpha-2 antagonism, autonomic opposition), not documented pramipexole-co-prescription interaction data.

#table(
  columns: (1.1fr, 1.6fr, 1.2fr, 1.6fr),
  table.header(
    [*Co-prescription*],
    [*Evidence for the pair*],
    [*Net direction*],
    [*ME/CFS note*],
  ),
  [Modafinil / armodafinil], [Samuels 2006: pramipexole 0.5 mg reduced alertness (pupillographic sleepiness, CFF) in healthy males while modafinil 200 mg did not; the pramipexole+modafinil combination arm was administered in the same double-blind crossover, but the reported results are the monotherapy results only @Samuels2006PramipexoleModafinil. Montastruc 2001: first record of modafinil co-prescribed to counter pramipexole-associated somnolence (PD pharmacovigilance comment) @Montastruc2001ModafinilPramipexole.], [Opposing arousal effects — the only pair in this table with quantified opposing-direction monotherapy effects; the combination itself is unmeasured (the offset assumes additivity, not tested)], [A rational offset of pramipexole sedation, untested in ME/CFS. Capped at open-question (@oq:pramipexole-modafinil-combo) — do not present as established.],
  [Caffeine], [No CYP-mediated PK interaction on the cited evidence: pramipexole is renally cleared (~80%), CYP-independent @Wright1997Pramipexolepk @Knop2015PramipexoleRenal (residual non-renal and renal-hemodynamic routes not excluded). Only association in the literature is pharmacodynamic in PD: >12 oz coffee/day associated with lower dyskinesia risk (HR 0.61, 95% CI 0.37–1.01 — CI crosses 1) via adenosine A2A antagonism @Wills2013CaffeineDyskinesia.], [None (PK); possible PD (A2A/D1), unproven], [Do not manage caffeine as a pramipexole exposure variable; see @cf:pramipexole-renal-clearance-caffeine.],
  [Midodrine], [No literature on the pair.], [Pharmacological opposition — pramipexole lowers BP (label OH risk), midodrine raises it], [Net BP effect unpredictable per patient; orthostatic vitals at baseline and each pramipexole titration step (see @pwarn:pramipexole-mecfs-safety).],
  [Fludrocortisone], [No literature on the pair.], [Volume expansion may offset DA-mediated vasodilation — inference only], [Same monitoring as midodrine; no interaction data exist to refine this.],
  [Beta-blockers (propranolol, atenolol)], [No literature on the pair.], [Masking — beta-blockade masks the compensatory tachycardia that warns of pramipexole-induced OH], [Unrecognised hypotension is the specific risk; same logic as the L-DOPA note in @warn:dopaminergic-augmentation-safety.],
  [Gabapentin / pregabalin], [Allen 2014: the 52-week RLS head-to-head — pregabalin 300 mg vs pramipexole 0.25/0.5 mg vs placebo (n = 719); augmentation over weeks 40–52 lower with pregabalin than pramipexole 0.5 mg (2.1% vs 7.7%, $p$ = 0.001) @Allen2014PregabalinPramipexole. Winkelman 2025: alpha-2-delta ligands are guideline first-line for chronic RLS @Winkelman2025RLSguideline.], [Class substitution, not simple addition — plus additive sedation (label) at therapeutic doses], [One of the frequent ME/CFS co-prescriptions (pain, sleep, or RLS); a given patient's chart may well already contain one. The first clinical question is usually "continue both, or substitute the dopamine agonist with the alpha-2-delta ligand (or vice versa)" — the guideline answer favours the alpha-2-delta ligand (@cf:rls-treatment-landscape).],
  [Antihistamines (diphenhydramine, cetirizine, hydroxyzine)], [No literature on the pair.], [Additive CNS depression — inference only], [Relevant mainly at the sedating end (diphenhydramine, hydroxyzine); cetirizine's contribution is small.],
  [Trazodone], [No literature on the pair.], [Additive sedation; both alter sleep architecture — inference only], [If pramipexole is dosed at night (IR) for RLS, the nocturnal sedation overlap is the practical concern.],
  [Amitriptyline (TCA)], [No literature on the pair.], [Additive sedation + anticholinergic load; TCAs add dopaminergic effects at higher doses — inference only], [Keep the TCA at its existing indication dose; do not titrate both agents simultaneously.],
  [LDN (low-dose naltrexone)], [No documented interaction.], [Independent opioid/TLR4/reward-circuit modulation — theoretical additive reward-circuit effect, untested (same caveat as @warn:dopaminergic-augmentation-safety)], [No PK overlap expected; no study has examined the pair.],
  [Pyridostigmine], [No documented interaction (cholinergic–dopaminergic balance effects noted theoretically for L-DOPA-induced dyskinesia in @warn:dopaminergic-augmentation-safety).], [None documented], [No study has examined the pair.],
