#open-question(title: [Does Modafinil Offset Pramipexole's Sedation in ME/CFS Without Eroding the RLS Benefit?])[
Modafinil (and armodafinil) is an established ME/CFS fatigue/cognitive agent in this paper (@sec:cns-stimulants), and pramipexole 0.5 mg reduces alertness in healthy volunteers while modafinil 200 mg does not — the pramipexole+modafinil combination arm was administered in the same double-blind crossover design, but the reported results are the monotherapy results only, so the offset is an inference from the monotherapy direction (assuming additivity), not a measured combination effect @Samuels2006PramipexoleModafinil. In the same volunteers pramipexole also dilated the resting pupil — a candidate autonomic readout of central D2 engagement (pupillometry as a catecholamine-probe, @spec:pupillometry-lc-readout). Pharmacovigilance records modafinil co-prescription specifically to counter pramipexole-associated somnolence in Parkinson's disease @Montastruc2001ModafinilPramipexole. In ME/CFS the question sharpens for three reasons: (1) ME/CFS patients with hypersomnia overlap (abnormal MSLT, high PLMS) are the highest-risk subgroup for pramipexole somnolence, so the offset has more to gain — and the combination's own sedation interaction means the offset may not be linear; (2) modafinil's own alertness mechanism (DAT/oriented arousal) converges on the same dopaminergic circuitry the paper treats as probe-sensitive — the stimulant action the paper localizes to arousal and reward-salience networks, not attention control (@spec:stimulant-arousal-reward-not-attention) — so the combination may itself be a dopaminergic state assay; (3) no study has examined the pair in any fatigue population, and the only quantification is healthy-volunteer pharmacodynamics @Samuels2006PramipexoleModafinil.

This item is capped at open-question per the drug-interaction pre-check: a documented opposing-direction interaction (sedation) exists, which blocks any stronger environment type until the pair is studied.

*Falsifiable reading of the existing data.* If the Montastruc co-prescription logic holds in ME/CFS, then an ME/CFS patient on pramipexole for RLS with daytime somnolence should show (a) reduced somnolence diary entries after modafinil addition, (b) preserved IRLS improvement, and (c) no change in the pramipexole side-effect profile beyond the arousal axis. Falsified if modafinil addition worsens total sleepiness (the sedation is not a reversible pharmacodynamic offset but an indicator of the patient's dopamine-state instability) or if IRLS worsens (the arousal axis and the RLS axis are coupled in this patient).
(Severity applicability: unknown — no data at any severity level.)
(Origin: drug-interaction inference from @Samuels2006PramipexoleModafinil + @Montastruc2001ModafinilPramipexole; no ME/CFS data.)

*Consequence:* A patient on both agents should track the two axes (RLS + daytime somnolence) separately through the first 2–4 weeks of the combination, and understand that the "modafinil fixes pramipexole sedation" pattern is documented only as a pharmacovigilance observation in Parkinson's disease — it is a hypothesis for ME/CFS, not an established interaction management rule.
] <oq:pramipexole-modafinil-combo>
